<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="trigsub" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Trigonometric substitution</title>

      <example>
      <p><em>This was Example 1 from the class notes.)</em>  Evaluate <m>\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2-25}}\,dx</m>.</p>
      <p><alert>(The following was written in red on the class notes, which is the color that signifies we are working with respect to <m>\theta</m>.)
	<md>
	   <mrow>\hbox{Let } x \amp = 5\sec\theta,</mrow>
	   <mrow>\hbox{ so that } dx \amp = 5 \sec\theta \tan\theta\ d\theta.</mrow>
	</md></alert></p>
<p>(And this was blue--a side note that was added after we antidifferentiated the <m>\theta</m>-term and immediately before we drew the right-triangle diagram.)  <em>NOTE that the "Let" statement is actually saying "Let <m>\theta</m> be <m>\sec^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{5}\right)</m>."</em>  And recall that this means that
	<ul>
		<li><m>\frac{x}{5} = \sec\theta</m></li>
		<li>AND (either <m>0 \leq \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m> or <m>\pi \leq \theta \lt \frac{3\pi}{2}</m>).</li>
	</ul>
	</p>
<p><alert>(Back to red ink now.)  The integral becomes
<md>
<mrow> \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{(5\sec\theta)^2 - 25}} \cdot 5 \sec\theta \tan\theta\ d\theta \amp = \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{5^2 \cdot (\sec^2\theta - 1)}} \cdot 5 \sec\theta \tan\theta\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{5^2} \cdot \sqrt{(\tan\theta)^2}} \cdot 5 \sec\theta \tan\theta\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \int \sec\theta\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \ln\left|\sec\theta + \tan\theta\right| + C. </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>

<p>(Blue ink again--a side note.)  Note that because of the restrictions on <m>\theta</m>, <m>\tan\theta</m> must be nonnegative, so that <m>\sqrt{(\tan\theta)^2} = \tan\theta</m>.  That was used in the above calculations to replace "<m>\sqrt{(\tan\theta)^2}</m>" with "<m>\tan\theta</m>."</p>

<p>NOW THE RIGHT-TRIANGLE DIAGRAM</p>

<p>NOW FINISHING UP BY PUTTING THINGS BACK INTO X-LANGUAGE</p>
<p>We rejoin the calculations above, already in progress:</p>
<p><alert>(We start out in red ink for the "<m>\theta</m>-language" and will switch to blue for the final step when we translate back to "<m>x</m>-language.")  The integral is
<md>
<mrow> \cdots \amp = \ln\left|\sec\theta + \tan\theta\right| + C </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \ln\left|\frac{x}{5} + \frac{\sqrt{x^2-25}}{5}\right| + C. </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>

<p>NOW THE ARBITRARY CONSTANT MASHUP</p>
      </example>

    <example>
      <p><em>This was Example 2 from the class notes.)</em>  Evaluate <m>\int \frac{\sqrt{9-x^2}}{x^2}\,dx</m>.</p>
      <p><alert>(The following was written in red on the class notes, which is the color that signifies we are working with respect to <m>\theta</m>.)
	<md>
	   <mrow>\hbox{Let } x \amp = 3\sin\theta,</mrow>
	   <mrow>\hbox{ so that } dx \amp = 3 \cos\theta\ d\theta.</mrow>
	</md></alert></p>
<p>(And this was blue--a side note that was added after we antidifferentiated the <m>\theta</m>-term and immediately before we drew the right-triangle diagram.)  <em>NOTE that the "Let" statement is actually saying "Let <m>\theta</m> be <m>\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{3}\right)</m>."</em>  And recall that this means that
	<ul>
		<li><m>\frac{x}{3} = \sin\theta</m></li>
		<li>AND <m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \leq \theta \leq \frac{\pi}{2}</m>.</li>
	</ul>
	</p>
<p><alert>(Back to red ink now.)  The integral becomes
<md>
<mrow> \int \frac{\sqrt{9-9\sin^2\theta}}{9\sin^2\theta} \cdot 3 \cos\theta\ d\theta \amp = \int \frac{\sqrt{9 \cdot (1 - \sin^2\theta)}}{9\sin^2\theta} \cdot 3 \cos\theta\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \int \frac{\sqrt{9} \cdot \sqrt{\cos^2\theta}}{9\sin^2\theta} \cdot 3 \cos\theta\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \int \frac{\cos^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \int \left(\frac{\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}\right)^2\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \int \cot^2\theta\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = \int (\csc^2\theta - 1)\ d\theta </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = -\cot\theta - \theta + C. </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>

<p>(Blue ink again--a side note.)  Note that because of the restrictions on <m>\theta</m>, <m>\cos\theta</m> must be nonnegative, so that <m>\sqrt{(\cos\theta)^2} = \cos\theta</m>.  That was used in the above calculations to replace "<m>\sqrt{(\cos\theta)^2}</m>" with "<m>\cos\theta</m>."</p>

<p>NOW THE RIGHT-TRIANGLE DIAGRAM</p>

<p>NOW FINISHING UP BY PUTTING THINGS BACK INTO X-LANGUAGE</p>
<p>We rejoin the calculations above, already in progress:</p>
<p><alert>(We start out in red ink for the "<m>\theta</m>-language" and will switch to blue for the final step when we translate back to "<m>x</m>-language.")  The integral is
<md>
<mrow> \cdots \amp = -\cot\theta - \theta + C </mrow>
<mrow> \amp = -\frac{\sqrt{9-x^2}}{x} - \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{x}{3}\right) + C. </mrow>
</md>
</alert></p>
      </example>

    <example>
      <p>Evaluate <m>\int \frac{1}{(x^2-6x+11)^2}\,dx</m>. Note <m>x^2-6x+11=(x-3)^2+2</m>. Let <m>x-3=\sqrt{2}	an \theta</m>, so <m>dx=\sqrt{2}\sec^2 \theta\,d \theta</m>.</p>
      <me>
        <m>\int \frac{1}{((x-3)^2+2)^2}\,dx= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{4}\int \cos^2 \theta\,d \theta= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{8}\left( \theta+	f\frac{\sin 2 \theta}{2}
ight)+C</m>
      </me>
      <p>Using <m>	an \theta= \frac{x-3}{\sqrt{2}}</m>, we obtain <m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{8} rctan\!\left(\frac{x-3}{\sqrt{2}}
ight)+\frac{x-3}{8((x-3)^2+2)}+C</m>.</p>
      </example>

    <example>
      <p>Find the appropriate substitution and identity for each integral. You may need to nearly complete the square.</p>

        <ul>
        <li>(a) <m>\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+4x^2}}\,dx</m>: let <m>2x=	an \theta</m> so <m>\sqrt{1+4x^2}=\sec \theta</m>.</li>
        <li>(b) <m>\int \frac{1}{x^2\sqrt{16-x^2}}\,dx</m>: let <m>x=4\sin \theta</m>; <todo>Check algebra with the <m>1/x^2</m> factor</todo>.</li>
        <li>(c) <m>\int \sqrt{x^2-9}\,dx</m>: let <m>x=3\sec \theta</m> so <m>\sqrt{x^2-9}=3	an \theta</m>.</li>
        <li>(d) <m>\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{9x^2+6x+1}}\,dx</m>: note <m>9x^2+6x+1=(3x+1)^2</m>. <todo>Confirm intended trig form vs. linear substitution</todo>.</li>
        </ul>
    </example>

</section>